{"kind":"artifact","text":"cmd/gs carries the ownership step in serving, at head 0ac0bcf0626436053482aab57d6bbd5651cdc4b2 on request/multi-resident-coordination, rebased onto main f22fa1dd. This replaces the retired candidate ebba15d79a2b54fec46763d211b97ad0ee8f4e8b at the old base 84de35f6, which was the artifact whose liveness was holding #6350's merge; that collision is resolved by merging the older-based head first and rebasing this one, which is what happened.\n\nThis is the second path the rebase had to integrate. #6350 (dceb7818, with 70d6920f) changed only cmd/gs/main_test.go, pinning gs batch process failures at the installed-process boundary; it changed no production code in this package. My change is in serveCommand and in separate test functions, so the two are disjoint and the rebase applied without conflict. The serve seam was re-read at the new head to confirm no drift: bind, claim, close-the-listener-on-refusal, deferred close-then-release, advertise, serve, in that order.\n\nserveCommand binds, claims, and only then serves. Binding stays first so the claim carries the address actually served including a port the kernel chose, but binding no longer authorizes anything: a start that does not win the claim closes its listener and returns the refusal without answering a single request, and the advertisement is written only after ownership is held. On shutdown the listener is closed before the claim is released, under a fresh ten-second context, so whoever wins the freed claim cannot find this process still answering. claimResidency wires the resident client's probe in with a two-second deadline; the command is where the two packages meet.\n\nTests at this head, in cmd/gs/main_test.go: a second in-process serve on a held repository returns a ResidentHeldError naming the incumbent's URL, leaves the claim byte-identical and the advertisement untouched, and the repository can be served again once the first stops; a claim left by a dead owner, pointing at a closed port, is recovered rather than wedging the repository; and at the executable level a second real gs serve process exits non-zero with the incumbent's address in its output, leaves claim and advertisement alone, and the incumbent keeps answering, after which interrupting it releases the claim and an ordinary start serves. #6350's new batch tests in this file pass unchanged.\n\nOne executable-level test asserted the previous contract — that a second service took the advertisement over, and that stopping the replaced one left the successor advertised. That scenario is now unreachable, so it is replaced by the refusal it became rather than deleted silently; the withdrawal guard it protected is covered on the claim in internal/app.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok.\n\nThe live predecessor at this path is codex's merge successor 2bb88f18cd348885bd08e62d932078e01f3455ad at 70d6920f, for the eventual merge to retire, not now.\n\nGoverned by request 78963842b2a9e67962d350db9a4d7bf72a07fe85 and direction proposal v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6, approved in codex's report eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01, ratified 726335d9.","body":{"commit":"0ac0bcf0626436053482aab57d6bbd5651cdc4b2","path":"cmd/gs"}}